window.OBJECT_IMAGES={"Sun":{"src":"assets/objects/sun.jpg","alt":"Sun - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Sun","file":"The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg","artist":"Mat\u00fa\u0161 Motlo","credit":"Own work","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","description":"The Sun photographed on the 8th of May, 2019 in white light (true color). Sunspots AR2740 (to the right) and AR2741 (to the left) visible. Some other interesting features include faculae, white spots near AR2741, limb darkening, and tiny convection cells called granules all across its surface.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Mercury":{"src":"assets/objects/mercury.jpg","alt":"Mercury - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Mercury (planet)","file":"Mercury_in_true_color.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercury_in_true_color.jpg","artist":"NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Arizona State University/Carnegie Institution of Washington","credit":"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA11364","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"This is a cropped bottom right image from the original four image mosaic PIA11364: Mercury's \"True\" Color is in the Eye of the Beholder. Original Caption Released with Image: Given the WAC\u2019s ability to take images through 11 narrow-band color filters, it is natural to wonder what does Mercury look like in \u201ctrue\u201d color such as would be seen by the human eye. However, creating such a natural color view is not as simple as it may seem. Shown here are four images of Mercury. The image in the top left is the previously released grayscale monochrome single WAC filter (430-nanometer) image (PIA11245); the remaining three images are three-color composites, produced by placing the same three WAC filter images with peak sensitivities at 480, 560, and 630 nanometers in the blue, green, and red channels, respectively. The differences between the color representations result from how the brightness and contrast of each individual WAC filter image was adjusted before it was combined into a color picture. In the top right view, all of the three filter images were stretched using the same brightness and contrast settings. In the bottom left picture, the brightness and contrast of each of the three filter images were determined independent of the others. In the bottom right, the brightness and contrast settings used in the upper right version were slightly adjusted to make each of the three filter images span a similar range of brightness and contrast values. So which color representation is \u201ccorrect\u201d for Mercury? The answer to that would indeed depend on the eye of the beholder. Every individual sees color differently; the human eye has a range of sensitivities that vary from person to person, resulting in different perceptions of \u201ctrue\u201d color. In addition, the three MDIS filter bands are narrow, and light at wavelengths between their peaks is not detected, unlike the human eye. In general, in light visible to the human eye, Mercury\u2019s surface shows only very subtle color variations, as seen in the three images here. However, when images from all 11 WAC filters are statistically compared and contrasted, these subtle color variations can be greatly enhanced, resulting in extremely colorful representations of Mercury\u2019s surface, such as seen in a high-resolution image of Th\u0101kur crater (PIA11365). Date Acquired: October 6, 2008 Image Mission Elapsed Time (MET): 131775256, 131775260, 131775264, 131775268 Instrument: Wide Angle Camera (WAC) of the Mercury Dual Imaging System (MDIS) Resolution: 5 kilometers/pixel (3 miles/pixel) Scale: Mercury\u2019s diameter is 4880 kilometers (3030 miles) Spacecraft Altitude: 27,000 kilometers (17,000 miles)","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Venus":{"src":"assets/objects/venus.jpg","alt":"Venus - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Venus","file":"Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg","artist":"NASA/JPL-Caltech","credit":"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/resources/2524/newly-processed-views-of-venus-from-mariner-10/","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"As it sped away from Venus, NASA's Mariner 10 spacecraft captured this seemingly peaceful view of a planet the size of Earth, wrapped in a dense, global cloud layer. But, contrary to its serene appearance, the clouded globe of Venus is a world of intense heat, crushing atmospheric pressure and clouds of corrosive acid.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Earth":{"src":"assets/objects/earth.jpg","alt":"Earth - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Earth","file":"The Earth seen from Apollo 17.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Earth_seen_from_Apollo_17.jpg","artist":"NASA/Apollo 17 crew; taken by either Harrison Schmitt or Ron Evans","credit":"https://web.archive.org/web/20160112123725/http://grin.hq.nasa.gov/ABSTRACTS/GPN-2000-001138.html (image link); see also https://www.nasa.gov/multimedia/imagegallery/image_feature_329.html","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"\"The Blue Marble\" is a famous photograph of the Earth taken on December 7, 1972, by the crew of the Apollo 17 spacecraft en route to the Moon at a distance of about 29,400 kilometres (18,300 mi). It shows Africa, Antarctica, and the Arabian Peninsula.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Mars":{"src":"assets/objects/mars.png","alt":"Mars - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Mars","file":"Mars_-_August_30_2021_-_Flickr_-_Kevin_M._Gill.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mars_-_August_30_2021_-_Flickr_-_Kevin_M._Gill.png","artist":"Kevin Gill from Los Angeles, CA, United States","credit":"Mars - August 30 2021","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","description":"Filters: f635, f546, f437 CU/LASP EMM/EXI ITF/Kevin M. Gill","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Jupiter":{"src":"assets/objects/jupiter.png","alt":"Jupiter - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Jupiter","file":"Jupiter_OPAL_2024.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jupiter_OPAL_2024.png","artist":"NASA/STSCI (S.T.A.R.S)","credit":"https://hubblesite.org/contents/media/images/2024/009/01HPMN3ZGXFN1E0M1W5Y8AVCAM?news=true","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Jupiter in true color by Hubble's \"Outer Planet Atmospheres Legacy\" (OPAL) - January 5 2024","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Saturn":{"src":"assets/objects/saturn.png","alt":"Saturn - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Saturn","file":"Saturn_global_view_from_Cassini,_rings_open_Better_Colour.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saturn_global_view_from_Cassini,_rings_open_Better_Colour.png","artist":"NASA / JPL-Caltech / SSI / color composite by Gordan Ugarkovic","credit":"https://www.planetary.org/space-images/saturn_globe_rings_open_ugodan","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Cassini captured the 30 frames required to construct this mosaic on 23 July 2008, from a distance of about a million kilometers. About a year before Saturn's equinox, the ring shadows have shifted from where they were early in Cassini's mission to a fat arc a little above the equator.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Uranus":{"src":"assets/objects/uranus.png","alt":"Uranus - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Uranus","file":"Uranus_Voyager2_color_calibrated.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uranus_Voyager2_color_calibrated.png","artist":"Ardenau4","credit":"Own workalso published on Flickr: https://www.flickr.com/photos/197038812@N04/53449450202/","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","description":"Uranus on 1986-01-23, taken by NASA's Voyager 2 probe. This color image was composed of three frames, orange, green, and blue, taken by Voyager 2's imaging system. This color image has been calibrated to best represent Uranus's true color and appearance. Based on: (in English) Irwin, Patrick G J (2023-12-23). \"Modelling the seasonal cycle of Uranus\u2019s colour and magnitude, and comparison with Neptune\". Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 527 (4): 11521\u201311538. DOI:10.1093/mnras/stad3761. ISSN 0035-8711.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Neptune":{"src":"assets/objects/neptune.png","alt":"Neptune - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Neptune","file":"Neptune_Voyager2_color_calibrated.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neptune_Voyager2_color_calibrated.png","artist":"NASA / Voyager 2 / PDS / OPUS / Ardenau4","credit":"Own workalso published on Flickr: https://www.flickr.com/photos/197038812@N04/53446862462/","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","description":"Neptune on 1989-08-17, taken by NASA's Voyager 2 probe. This color image was composed of three frames, orange, green, and blue, taken by Voyager 2's imaging system. This color image has been calibrated to best represent Neptune's true color and appearance. Based on: (in English) Irwin, Patrick G J (2023-12-23). \"Modelling the seasonal cycle of Uranus\u2019s colour and magnitude, and comparison with Neptune\". Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 527 (4): 11521\u201311538. DOI:10.1093/mnras/stad3761. ISSN 0035-8711.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Pluto":{"src":"assets/objects/pluto.png","alt":"Pluto - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Pluto","file":"Pluto_in_True_Color_-_High-Res.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pluto_in_True_Color_-_High-Res.png","artist":"NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Southwest Research Institute/Alex Parker","credit":"http://pluto.jhuapl.edu/Galleries/Featured-Images/image.php?page=1&gallery_id=2&image_id=543","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Three years after NASA's New Horizons spacecraft gave humankind our first close-up views of Pluto and its largest moon, Charon, scientists are still revealing the wonders of these incredible worlds in the outer solar system. Marking the anniversary of New Horizons' historic flight through the Pluto system on July 14, 2015, mission scientists released the highest-resolution color images of Pluto and Charon. These natural-color images result from refined calibration of data gathered by New Horizons' color Multispectral Visible Imaging Camera (MVIC). The processing creates images that would approximate the colors that the human eye would perceive, bringing them closer to \u201ctrue color\u201d than the images released near the encounter. This image was taken as New Horizons zipped toward Pluto and its moons on July 14, 2015, from a range of 22,025 miles (35,445) kilometers. This single color MVIC scan includes no data from other New Horizons imagers or instruments added. The striking features on Pluto are clearly visible, including the bright expanse of Pluto's icy, nitrogen-and-methane rich \"heart,\" Sputnik Planitia.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Charon":{"src":"assets/objects/charon.png","alt":"Charon - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Charon (moon)","file":"Charon_in_True_Color_-_High-Res.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charon_in_True_Color_-_High-Res.png","artist":"NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Southwest Research Institute/Alex Parker","credit":"http://pluto.jhuapl.edu/Multimedia/Science-Photos/image.php?page=1&gallery_id=2&image_id=544","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Three years after NASA's New Horizons spacecraft gave humankind our first close-up views of Pluto and its largest moon, Charon, scientists are still revealing the wonders of these incredible worlds in the outer solar system. Marking the anniversary of New Horizons' historic flight through the Pluto system on July 14, 2015, mission scientists released the highest-resolution color images of Pluto and Charon. These natural-color images result from refined calibration of data gathered by New Horizons' color Multispectral Visible Imaging Camera (MVIC). The processing creates images that would approximate the colors that the human eye would perceive, bringing them closer to \u201ctrue color\u201d than the images released near the encounter. This image was taken on July 14, 2015, from a range of 46,091 miles (74,176 kilometers). This single color MVIC scan includes no data from other New Horizons imagers or instruments added. The striking features on Charon are clearly visible, including the reddish north-polar region known as Mordor Macula.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Moon":{"src":"assets/objects/moon.jpg","alt":"Moon - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Moon","file":"FullMoon2010.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FullMoon2010.jpg","artist":"Gregory H. Revera","credit":"Own work","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","description":"Full Moon photograph taken 10-22-2010 from Madison, Alabama, USA. Photographed with a Celestron 9.25 Schmidt-Cassegrain telescope. Acquired with a Canon EOS Rebel T1i (EOS 500D), 20 images stacked to reduce noise. 200 ISO 1/640 sec.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Phobos":{"src":"assets/objects/phobos.jpg","alt":"Phobos - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Phobos (moon)","file":"Phobos_colour_2008.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phobos_colour_2008.jpg","artist":"NASA / JPL-Caltech / University of Arizona","credit":"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA10368","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Color image of Phobos, imaged by the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter on 23 March 2008. The High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) camera on NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter took two images of the larger of Mars' two moons, Phobos, within 10 minutes of each other on 23 March 2008. This is the first, taken from a distance of about 6,800 kilometers (about 4,200 miles). It is presented in color by combining data from the camera's blue-green, red, and near-infrared channels. The illuminated part of Phobos seen in the images is about 21 kilometers (13 miles) across. The most prominent feature in the images is the large crater Stickney in the lower right. With a diameter of 9 kilometers (5.6 miles), it is the largest feature on Phobos. The color data accentuate details not apparent in black-and-white images. For example, materials near the rim of Stickney appear bluer than the rest of Phobos. Based on analogy with materials on our own moon, this could mean this surface is fresher, and therefore younger, than other parts of Phobos. A series of troughs and crater chains is obvious on other parts of the moon. Although many appear radial to Stickney in this image, recent studies from the European Space Agency's Mars Express orbiter indicate that they are not related to Stickney. Instead, they may have formed when material ejected from impacts on Mars later collided with Phobos. The lineated textures on the walls of Stickney and other large craters are landslides formed from materials falling into the crater interiors in the weak Phobos gravity (less than one one-thousandth of the gravity on Earth). In the full-resolution version of this image, a pixel encompasses 6.8 meters (22 feet), providing a resolution (smallest visible feature) of about 20 meters (about 65 feet). The image is in the HiRISE catalog as PSP_007769_9010. NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, a division of the California Institute of Technology in Pasadena, manages the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter for NASA's Science Mission Directorate, Washington. Lockheed Martin Space Systems, Denver, is the prime contractor for the project and built the spacecraft. The High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment is operated by the University of Arizona, Tucson, and the instrument was built by Ball Aerospace & Technologies Corp., Boulder, Colo.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Deimos":{"src":"assets/objects/deimos.jpg","alt":"Deimos - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Deimos (moon)","file":"NASA-Deimos-MarsMoon-20090221.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Deimos-MarsMoon-20090221.jpg","artist":"NASA/JPL/University of Arizona","credit":"http://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/images/2009/deimos/ESP_012068_9000_COLOR5.tif","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Deimos, Moon of Mars ESP_012068_9000 - 2nd Observation (21 February 2009) http://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/deimos.php HiRISE captured these enhanced-color images of Deimos, the smaller of the two moons of Mars, on 21 February 2009. Deimos has a smooth surface due to a blanket of fragmental rock or regolith, except for the most recent impact craters. It is a dark, reddish object, very similar to Phobos, shown here in enhanced HiRISE colors (near-infrared, red, and blue-green). HiRISE took images of Phobos on 23 March 2008. There are subtle color variations\u2014redder in the smoothest areas and less red near fresh impact craters and over ridges or topographic highs (relative to its center of gravity). These color variations are probably caused by the exposure of surface materials to the space environment, which leads to darkening and reddening. Brighter and less-red surface materials have seen less exposure to space due to recent impacts or downslope movement of regolith. With an image scale of about 20 meters/pixel, features 60 m or larger can be discerned. The images were acquired 5 hrs 35 minutes apart, so the sun was to the upper left in the first (left) image and to the right in the second image. Although the viewing geometry is similar in the two images, surface features appear very different due to the changes in illumination. EDR Products EDR Products for ESP_012068_9000 - 2nd Observation (21 February 2009) EDR Products for ESP_012065_9000 - 1st Observation (21 February 2009) Written by: Alfred McEwen (9 March 2009) NOTE - Also See the following website references for the location of \"Swift crater\" and \"Voltaire crater\" on the surface of Deimos (the smaller of two moons of planet Mars). http://planetary.org/blogs/emily-lakdawalla/2009/1868.html http://elsofista.blogspot.com/2009/03/deimos-la-pequena-luna-marciana.html NOTE - Converted Original TIF Image to JPG (via MS Paint v5.1) and Rotated 180 degrees clockwise (via ACDSee Photo Manager 2009) - and uploaded to Wikimedia Commons.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Io":{"src":"assets/objects/io.png","alt":"Io - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Io (moon)","file":"Io_highest_resolution_natural_color.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Io_highest_resolution_natural_color.png","artist":"NASA","credit":"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA02308","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"NASA's Galileo spacecraft acquired its highest resolution images of Jupiter's moon Io on 3 July 1999 during its closest pass to Io since orbit insertion in late 1995. This color mosaic uses the near-infrared, green and violet filters (slightly more than the visible range) of the spacecraft's camera and approximates what the human eye would see. Most of Io's surface has pastel colors, punctuated by black, brown, green, orange, and red units near the active volcanic centers. A false color version of the mosaic has been created to enhance the contrast of the color variations. The improved resolution reveals small-scale color units which had not been recognized previously and which suggest that the lavas and sulfurous deposits are composed of complex mixtures (Cutout A of false color image). Some of the bright (whitish), high-latitude (near the top and bottom) deposits have an ethereal quality like a transparent covering of frost (Cutout B of false color image). Bright red areas were seen previously only as diffuse deposits. However, they are now seen to exist as both diffuse deposits and sharp linear features like fissures (Cutout C of false color image). Some volcanic centers have bright and colorful flows, perhaps due to flows of sulfur rather than silicate lava (Cutout D of false color image). In this region bright, white material can also be seen to emanate from linear rifts and cliffs. Comparison of this image to previous Galileo images reveals many changes due to the ongoing volcanic activity. Galileo will make two close passes of Io beginning in October of this year. Most of the high-resolution targets for these flybys are seen on the hemisphere shown here. North is to the top of the picture and the sun illuminates the surface from almost directly behind the spacecraft. This illumination geometry is good for imaging color variations, but poor for imaging topographic shading. However, some topographic shading can be seen here due to the combination of relatively high resolution (1.3 kilometers or 0.8 miles per picture element) and the rugged topography over parts of Io. The image is centered at 0.3 degrees north latitude and 137.5 degrees west longitude. The resolution is 1.3 kilometers (0.8 miles) per picture element. The images were taken on 3 July 1999 at a range of about 130,000 kilometers (81,000 miles) by the Solid State Imaging (SSI) system on NASA's Galileo spacecraft during its twenty-first orbit. The Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Pasadena, CA manages the Galileo mission for NASA's Office of Space Science, Washington, DC. This image and other images and data received from Galileo are posted on the World Wide Web, on the Galileo mission home page at URL http://galileo.jpl.nasa.gov. Background information and educational context for the images can be found at URL http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/galileo/sepo.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Europa":{"src":"assets/objects/europa.png","alt":"Europa - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Europa (moon)","file":"Europa_-_Perijove_45_(cropped).png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europa_-_Perijove_45_(cropped).png","artist":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS/Kevin M. Gill","credit":"File:Europa - Perijove 45 (53255790801).png, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/kevinmgill/53255790801","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","description":"This is Europa in true color, cropped from Juno's flyby of Europa at Perijove 45.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Ganymede":{"src":"assets/objects/ganymede.png","alt":"Ganymede - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Ganymede (moon)","file":"Ganymede_-_Perijove_34_Composite.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganymede_-_Perijove_34_Composite.png","artist":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS/Kevin M. Gill","credit":"Ganymede - Perijove 34 Composite","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","description":"Ganymede photographed by Juno in 2021, Projected from the perspective of '3.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Callisto":{"src":"assets/objects/callisto.png","alt":"Callisto - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Callisto (moon)","file":"Callisto_VGR2_C2060635_OGB.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Callisto_VGR2_C2060635_OGB.png","artist":"zelario12","credit":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/197038812@N04/54002253611/","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","description":"From Voyager 2. Large mosaic of the moon Callisto made from 9 separate clear filter narrow-angle images. Color was added using four lower-resolution wide-angle frames in the orange, green, blue, and violet filters. Color is calibrated against spectral data and is close to the natural color of Callisto.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Rhea":{"src":"assets/objects/rhea.jpg","alt":"Rhea - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Rhea (moon)","file":"PIA07763_Rhea_full_globe5.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA07763_Rhea_full_globe5.jpg","artist":"NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute","credit":"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA07763","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"This giant mosaic reveals Saturn's icy moon Rhea in her full, crater-scarred glory. This view consists of 21 clear-filter images and is centered at 0.4 degrees south latitude, 171 degrees west longitude. The giant impact basin Tirawa is seen above and to the right of center. Tirawa, and the even larger basin Mamaldi to its southwest, are both covered in impact craters, indicating they are quite ancient. The bright, approximately 40-kilometer-wide (25-mile) ray crater seen in many Cassini views of Rhea is located on the right side of this mosaic (at 12 degrees south latitude, 111 degrees west longitude). See PIA07764 for a close-up view of the eastern portion of the bright, ray crater. There are few signs of tectonic activity in this view. However, the wispy streaks on Rhea that were seen at lower resolution by NASA's Voyager and Cassini spacecraft, were beyond the western (left) limb from this perspective. In high-resolution Cassini flyby images of Dione, similar features were identified as fractures caused by extensive tectonism. Rhea is Saturn's second-largest moon, at 1,528 kilometers (949 miles) across. The images in this mosaic were taken with the Cassini spacecraft narrow-angle camera during a close flyby on Nov. 26, 2005. The images were acquired as Cassini approached the moon at distances ranging from 79,190 to 58,686 kilometers (49,206 to 36,466 miles) from Rhea and at a Sun-Rhea-spacecraft, or phase, angle of about 19 degrees. Image scale in the mosaic is 354 meters (1,161 feet) per pixel. The Cassini-Huygens mission is a cooperative project of NASA, the European Space Agency and the Italian Space Agency. The Jet Propulsion Laboratory, a division of the California Institute of Technology in Pasadena, manages the mission for NASA's Science Mission Directorate, Washington, D.C. The Cassini orbiter and its two onboard cameras were designed, developed and assembled at JPL. The imaging operations center is based at the Space Science Institute in Boulder, Colo. For more information about the Cassini-Huygens mission visit http://saturn.jpl.nasa.gov. The Cassini imaging team homepage is at http://ciclops.org. The NASA image has been processed to enhance contrast and had black panels added to its borders.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Titan":{"src":"assets/objects/titan.jpg","alt":"Titan - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Titan (moon)","file":"Titan_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titan_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg","artist":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/SSI/Kevin M. Gill","credit":"File:Titan - December 16 2011 (40047599334).jpg by [1]","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","description":"I've taken a slightly larger image that is processed by Kevin M. Gill, with far less noise. Titan's color in this image also more closely match with its spectra color.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Iapetus":{"src":"assets/objects/iapetus.png","alt":"Iapetus - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Iapetus (moon)","file":"Iapetus_as_seen_by_Cassini_True_Color.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus_as_seen_by_Cassini_True_Color.png","artist":"Anonymsiy","credit":"Own work","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"A true colour version of this image: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus_as_seen_by_the_Cassini_probe_-_20071008.jpg . Processed using True Color Tools and then calibrated using Gimp using TCT's database viewer as reference.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Titania":{"src":"assets/objects/titania.png","alt":"Titania - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Titania (moon)","file":"Titania_VGR2_C2684315.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titania_VGR2_C2684315.png","artist":"zelario12","credit":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/197038812@N04/53515363728/","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","description":"two clear frames stacked and sharpened. Colorized using a GCV color composite obtained earlier in the flyby. Color is calibrated against spectra and is close to true color.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Oberon":{"src":"assets/objects/oberon.jpg","alt":"Oberon - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Oberon (moon)","file":"Oberon_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oberon_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg","artist":"Kevin M. Gill","credit":"File:Oberon - Green-Violet - January 24 1986 - 4 (50906003243).jpg, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/kevinmgill/50906003243/","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","description":"Processed using green, violet, and unfiltered images of Oberon (moon of Uranus) taken by Voyager 2 on January 24 1986. Original data is taken from NASA/JPL-Caltech.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Triton":{"src":"assets/objects/triton.jpg","alt":"Triton - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Triton (moon)","file":"Triton-usgs23-with-frame.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triton-usgs23-with-frame.jpg","artist":"Michael T. Bland, U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","credit":"https://www.sciencebase.gov/catalog/item/6515fe80d34e469cabfce1bb https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/PSJ/ad33ca#psjad33cas6","license":"CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","description":"A full-disc mosaic of Triton","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Voyager 1":{"src":"assets/objects/voyager-1.png","alt":"Voyager 1 - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Voyager 1","file":"Voyager_spacecraft_model.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voyager_spacecraft_model.png","artist":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","credit":"Published source: \"[1]\" at NASA ScienceDirect source: Image hosted by smd-prod.s3.amazonaws.com.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Artist's rendering, from NASA, of the design employed by the twin Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 spacecraft, in mission configuration. Sent into the outer solar system for the purpose of embarking on the \"grand tour\" of the Gas giants, Voyager 1 made successful reconnaissance flybys of the planet Jupiter and Saturn in 1979 and 1980. Voyager 2 also performed flybys of Jupiter and Saturn in 1979 and 1981, along with the very first, and so far only, visits of the planets Uranus and Neptune in 1986 and 1989.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Voyager 2":{"src":"assets/objects/voyager-2.png","alt":"Voyager 2 - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Voyager 2","file":"Voyager_spacecraft_model.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voyager_spacecraft_model.png","artist":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","credit":"Published source: \"[1]\" at NASA ScienceDirect source: Image hosted by smd-prod.s3.amazonaws.com.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Artist's rendering, from NASA, of the design employed by the twin Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 spacecraft, in mission configuration. Sent into the outer solar system for the purpose of embarking on the \"grand tour\" of the Gas giants, Voyager 1 made successful reconnaissance flybys of the planet Jupiter and Saturn in 1979 and 1980. Voyager 2 also performed flybys of Jupiter and Saturn in 1979 and 1981, along with the very first, and so far only, visits of the planets Uranus and Neptune in 1986 and 1989.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Pioneer 10":{"src":"assets/objects/pioneer-10.jpg","alt":"Pioneer 10 - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Pioneer 10","file":"Pioneer_10_(18427352949).jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pioneer_10_(18427352949).jpg","artist":"NASA on The Commons","credit":"Pioneer 10","license":"No restrictions","licenseUrl":"https://www.flickr.com/commons/usage/","description":"On June 13, 1983, Pioneer 10 passed beyond Neptune\u2019s orbit, becoming first man-made object to leave the Solar System.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Pioneer 11":{"src":"assets/objects/pioneer-11.jpg","alt":"Pioneer 11 - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Pioneer 11","file":"An_artist's_impression_of_a_Pioneer_spacecraft_on_its_way_to_interstellar_space.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_artist%27s_impression_of_a_Pioneer_spacecraft_on_its_way_to_interstellar_space.jpg","artist":"NASA/Don Davis","credit":"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/ames/news/2013/pioneer11-40-years.html","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"An artist's impression of Pioneer 10 looking back on the inner Solar while on its way to interstellar space.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"New Horizons":{"src":"assets/objects/new-horizons.png","alt":"New Horizons - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"New Horizons","file":"New_Horizons_spacecraft_model_1.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horizons_spacecraft_model_1.png","artist":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), Applied Physics Laboratory","credit":"\"PEPSSI Instrument Tastes Pluto's Atmosphere\" from the Applied Physics Laboratory New Horizons website.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"A transparent image of the New Horizons spacecraft.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Parker Solar Probe":{"src":"assets/objects/parker-solar-probe.png","alt":"Parker Solar Probe - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Parker Solar Probe","file":"Parker_Solar_Probe_spacecraft_model.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parker_Solar_Probe_spacecraft_model.png","artist":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","credit":"Cropped from an image hosted by nasa.gov","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Transparent background image of the Parker Solar Probe spacecraft, for use in Wikipedia infoboxes.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"James Webb Space Telescope":{"src":"assets/objects/james-webb-space-telescope.png","alt":"James Webb Space Telescope - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"James Webb Space Telescope","file":"JWST_spacecraft_model_3.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JWST_spacecraft_model_3.png","artist":"NASA","credit":"https://www.jwst.nasa.gov/content/webbLaunch/whereIsWebb.html","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"The James Webb Space Telescope (sometimes called JWST or Webb) is an orbiting infrared observatory that complements and extends the discoveries of the Hubble Space Telescope, with longer wavelength coverage and greatly improved sensitivity. The longer wavelengths enable Webb to look much closer to the beginning of time and to hunt for the unobserved formation of the first galaxies, as well as to look inside dust clouds where stars and planetary systems are forming today.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Lucy":{"src":"assets/objects/lucy.png","alt":"Lucy - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Lucy (spacecraft)","file":"Lucy-PatroclusMenoetius-art.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucy-PatroclusMenoetius-art.png","artist":"NASA\u2019s Goddard Space Flight Center/Conceptual Image Lab/Adriana Gutierrez","credit":"http://lucy.swri.edu/img/1000days.png http://lucy.swri.edu/2019/01/20/1000days.html","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"This artist\u2019s concept depicts the Lucy spacecraft flying past the Trojan asteroid (617) Patroclus and its binary companion Menoetius. Lucy will be the first mission to explore Jupiter\u2019s Trojan asteroids \u2013 ancient remnants of the outer solar system trapped in the giant planet\u2019s orbit.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Psyche":{"src":"assets/objects/psyche.jpg","alt":"Psyche - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Psyche (spacecraft)","file":"PIA21499_-_Artist's_Concept_of_Psyche_Spacecraft_with_Five-Panel_Array.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA21499_-_Artist%27s_Concept_of_Psyche_Spacecraft_with_Five-Panel_Array.jpg","artist":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Arizona State Univ./Space Systems Loral/Peter Rubin","credit":"This image or video was catalogued by Jet Propulsion Laboratory of the United States National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) under Photo ID: PIA21499.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"This artist's-concept illustration depicts the spacecraft of NASA's Psyche mission near the mission's target, the metal asteroid Psyche. The artwork was created in May 2017 to show the five-panel solar arrays planned for the spacecraft. The spacecraft's structure will include power and propulsion systems to travel to, and orbit, the asteroid. These systems will combine solar power with electric propulsion to carry the scientific instruments used to study the asteroid through space. The mission plans launch in 2022 and arrival at Psyche, between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter, in 2026. This selected asteroid is made almost entirely of nickel-iron metal. It offers evidence about violent collisions that created Earth and other terrestrial planets.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Europa Clipper":{"src":"assets/objects/europa-clipper.png","alt":"Europa Clipper - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Europa Clipper","file":"Europa_Clipper_spacecraft_model.png","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europa_Clipper_spacecraft_model.png","artist":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) \u00b7 Jet Propulsion Laboratory","credit":"Published source: Europa Mission Spacecraft - Artist's Rendering \" by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.Direct source: Image hosted by jpl.nasa.gov.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Transparent background version of the Europa Clipper spacecraft rendering, per the official JPL website.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Halley":{"src":"assets/objects/halley.jpg","alt":"Halley - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Halley's Comet","file":"Lspn_comet_halley.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lspn_comet_halley.jpg","artist":"NASA/W. Liller","credit":"NSSDC's Photo Gallery (NASA): http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/photo_gallery/photogallery-comets.html http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/image/planetary/comet/lspn_comet_halley1.jpg","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"Comet 1P/Halley as taken March 8, 1986 by W. Liller, Easter Island, part of the International Halley Watch (IHW) Large Scale Phenomena Network.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Hale-Bopp":{"src":"assets/objects/hale-bopp.jpg","alt":"Hale-Bopp - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Comet Hale-Bopp","file":"Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg","artist":"E. Kolmhofer, H. Raab; Johannes-Kepler-Observatory, Linz, Austria (https://sternwarte.at)","credit":"Own work","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","description":"An image of comet C/1995 O1 (Hale-Bopp), taken on April 04, 1997 with a 225mm f/2.0 Schmidt Camera (focal length 450mm) on Kodak Panther 400 color slide film with an exposure time of 10 minutes; the field shown is about 6.5\u00b0x6.5\u00b0; at full resolution, the stars in the image appear slightly elongated, as the camera tracked the comet during the exposure","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"Encke":{"src":"assets/objects/encke.jpg","alt":"Encke - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Comet Encke","file":"Comet_Encke.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_Encke.jpg","artist":"Jim Scotti","credit":"http://neo.jpl.nasa.gov/images/encke.htmlhttps://web.archive.org/web/19991008161837/http://neo.jpl.nasa.gov/images/encke.html","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","description":"This is an image of short-period comet Encke obtained by Jim Scotti on 1994 January 5 while using the 0.91-meter Spacewatch Telescope on Kitt Peak. The image is 9.18 arcminutes square with north on the right and east at top. The integration time is 150 seconds.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko":{"src":"assets/objects/67p-churyumov-gerasimenko.jpg","alt":"67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko","file":"Comet_67P_True_color.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_67P_True_color.jpg","artist":"ESA/Rosetta/MPS for OSIRIS Team MPS/UPD/LAM/IAA/SSO/INTA/UPM/DASP/IDA","credit":"https://blogs.esa.int/rosetta/2014/12/12/comet-67pc-g-in-living-colour/ https://www.planetary.org/space-images/comet-cg-20140806-osiris-color https://www.flickr.com/photos/europeanspaceagency/15978891926/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","description":"A colour image of Comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko composed of three images taken with the Narrow Angle Camera (NAC) of the scientific imaging system OSIRIS in red (centred at 744 nm wavelength), green (536 nm), and blue (481 nm) filters on 6 August 2014 from a distance of 120 kilometres. The image covers roughly 4 x 4 km at a resolution of about 3.9 metres per pixel. Rosetta\u2019s OSIRIS team have produced a colour image of Comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko as it would be seen by the human eye. As anticipated, the comet turns out to be very grey indeed, with only slight, subtle colour variations seen across its surface. To create an image revealing 67P\u2019s \u201ctrue\u201d colours, the scientists superposed images taken sequentially through filters centred on red, green, and blue wavelengths. \u201cAs it turns out, 67P/C-G looks dark grey, in reality almost as black as coal,\u201d says the instrument\u2019s Principal Investigator Holger Sierks from the Max Planck Institute for Solar System Research (MPS). As explained in earlier blog posts for the NAVCAM images, the intensity of the images has been enhanced to span the full range from black to white, in order to make surface details visible. But the colours have not been enhanced: the comet really is very grey. A more detailed first analysis nevertheless reveals that the comet reflects red light slightly more efficiently than other wavelengths. This is a well-known phenomenon observed at many other small bodies in the Solar System and is due to the small size of the surface grains. That does not, however, mean that the comet would look red to the human eye. Natural sunlight peaks in the green part of the spectrum and the response of the human eye is similarly matched. Thus, overall, the comet would look rather grey to the human eye, as seen here.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."},"NEOWISE":{"src":"assets/objects/neowise.jpg","alt":"NEOWISE - Wikipedia lead image (may be an artist's illustration)","title":"Comet NEOWISE","file":"Comet_2020_F3_(NEOWISE)_on_Jul_14_2020_aligned_to_stars.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_2020_F3_(NEOWISE)_on_Jul_14_2020_aligned_to_stars.jpg","artist":"SimgDe","credit":"Own work","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","description":"Image is aligned to stars. Total exposure time was 15 min.","changes":"Wikimedia thumbnail scaled to 480 pixels; no other changes."}};
